.TH "jaro" "1" "" "" ""
.SH jaro(1)
.SS Name
jaro \- command line interface for Jaromail workflows
.SS Synopsis
\f[CR]jaro [options] [command] [command\-options]\f[R]
.SS Description
\f[CR]jaro\f[R] is the operational CLI for Jaromail.
It orchestrates account configuration, local maildir operations,
addressbooks, filtering, indexing and SMTP/IMAP actions.
Contacts live in \f[CR]contacts.sqlite\f[R] in the JaroMail directory
and are shared across its accounts.
Recipient completion searches allow and custom lists.
.SS Options
\f[CR]\-a ACCOUNT\f[R] use a specific account instead of
\f[CR]default\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]\-l LIST\f[R] select a contact list by exact name (default:
\f[CR]allow\f[R]).
.PP
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] dry\-run mode where supported.
.PP
\f[CR]\-q\f[R] quiet mode.
.PP
\f[CR]\-D\f[R] debug mode.
.PP
\f[CR]\-h\f[R] show help.
.PP
\f[CR]\-v\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-version\f[R] prints the assigned release
version and exits.
The startup banner and help use the same installed metadata.
Source builds derive it from release tags, with the v6.0.0 development
baseline when no v6 tag exists.
.PP
\f[CR]\-f\f[R] force mode for selected operations.
.SS Filter engine selection
Pigeonhole executes local filters.
The default and the \f[CR]sieve\f[R], \f[CR]native\f[R] and
\f[CR]legacy\f[R] values of \f[CR]JARO_FILTER_ENGINE\f[R] select the
same installed runtime.
Run \f[CR]jaro fresh\-filters\f[R] to migrate an older generation from
your existing \f[CR]Filters.txt\f[R], aliases, accounts and
addressbooks.
Until then, filter and fetch report the required update before touching
messages or starting transport.
An unsuccessful migration preserves the last coherent policy and all
mail.
Historical messages stay in \f[CR]known\f[R] and \f[CR]zz.spam\f[R].
New known and spam deliveries use \f[CR]INBOX\f[R] and \f[CR]Spam\f[R]
by default.
Custom Sieve mailbox mappings apply locally and to the exported script.
Existing files in the destination Maildir are preserved.
.PP
\f[CR]jaro fresh\-filters\f[R] publishes the shared local generation and
portable Sieve export.
\f[CR]jaro sieve\f[R] can also regenerate the policy after migration.
\f[CR]jaro filter [MAILDIR]\f[R] applies it to incoming mail.
\f[CR]jaro fetch\f[R] applies it after transport and refreshes the index
once after the batch, including partial failures.
Filter alone does not index.
\f[CR]jaro filter \-\-explain [MAILDIR|MESSAGE]\f[R],
\f[CR]jaro \-n filter\f[R] and \f[CR]jaro \-n fetch\f[R] inspect the
selected generation without publication, delivery or indexing.
A missing or stale generation requires \f[CR]jaro fresh\-filters\f[R]
before preview.
A fresh \f[CR]jaro init\f[R] installs commented typed examples and
active builtins.
Supported legacy EREs work with the default profile.
.PP
Native \f[CR]filter \-\-compare FILE [MAILDIR]\f[R] compares retained
actual final legacy observations against native classification.
It never applies either engine.
FILE uses complete fingerprint\-bound JLC1 NUL framing described in
\f[CR]filter\-interfaces.md\f[R].
Its producer must retain unchanged\-corpus hashes and actual legacy
provenance.
Ambiguous dry\-run proposals and unsupported files fail; this command
does not infer a legacy winner from proposal text.
.SS Commands
.SS Core mail flow
\f[CR]doctor [\-\-json]\f[R] report local setup problems without
initialization, network requests, credential retrieval or repair.
\f[CR]jaro \-a ACCOUNT doctor\f[R] checks a selected account.
It continues through independently discoverable problems even if mail
tools or version metadata are missing.
Exit status is 0 when there are no blocking findings, 1 for actionable
blockers, and 2 for invalid invocation or an internal diagnostic
failure.
Human output suggests commands without running them.
\f[CR]\-\-json\f[R] emits schema version 1 with stable check IDs,
status, redacted \f[CR]explanation\f[R] and \f[CR]next_action\f[R]
strings, resolved paths and \f[CR]exit_status\f[R].
Warnings and unknown checks do not make the exit status nonzero.
See the manual\[cq]s offline diagnostics section for the complete schema
and setup flow.
.PP
\f[CR]wizard\f[R] interactive account setup.
.PP
\f[CR]fetch\f[R] fetch unread messages for an account.
.PP
\f[CR]send\f[R] send queued messages from \f[CR]outbox/\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]outbox list\f[R] print stable queue IDs, delivery states and
classified outcomes without message content or recipients.
This route does not initialize files, consult credentials or require the
mail transport tools.
.PP
\f[CR]outbox retry ID \-\-confirm\-duplicate\f[R] explicitly retry only
a held uncertain item.
The required flag acknowledges possible duplicate delivery.
\f[CR]\-a ACCOUNT\f[R] selects its account.
A new uncertain result is held again.
.PP
\f[CR]outbox archive ID\f[R] record externally verified delivery and
complete the local sent copy without SMTP or account/credential access.
Accepted, verified and complete items can also finish local recovery
this way.
.PP
Both actions accept \f[CR]\-n\f[R] for validation without writes or
transport.
Unknown, stale, ambiguous and content\-mismatched IDs are refused.
See delivery recovery for states, examples and damaged\-state handling.
Bare \f[CR]outbox\f[R] still opens the outbox Maildir in Mutt.
.PP
\f[CR]queue\f[R] read a full message from stdin and queue it.
.PP
\f[CR]smtp\f[R] read a full message from stdin and send via SMTP.
.PP
\f[CR]peek\f[R] open remote IMAP mailbox view.
.PP
\f[CR]open\f[R] open a local maildir folder.
.PP
\f[CR]serve\f[R] expose local Maildirs through Dovecot 2.4.x on
\f[CR]127.0.0.1:61443\f[R].
Run as the mail owner.
Same\-user clients can read the mode\-0600
\f[CR]MAIL_ROOT/.imap/passwd\f[R] credential file, containing
\f[CR]username:{PLAIN}password\f[R].
The \f[CR]Credentials:\f[R] startup line gives its path.
\f[CR]JARO_SERVE_USER\f[R] defaults to \f[CR]jaro\f[R];
\f[CR]JARO_SERVE_PASSWORD\f[R] overrides the otherwise randomly
generated password.
Read the current file for each new server session.
See the manual\[cq]s local IMAP section for credential lifetime and
client behavior.
.SS IMAP folder shell
Start \f[CR]jaro \-a ACCOUNT imap shell\f[R] from the directory where
you want the archive.
Choose the account in \f[CR]$JAROMAILDIR/Accounts/\f[R].
The shell uses its usual login and keyring.
Set \f[CR]proto imap\f[R], \f[CR]imap HOST\f[R],
\f[CR]imap_port 993\f[R], \f[CR]transport implicit\f[R] and
\f[CR]cert check\f[R] for verified implicit TLS.
For STARTTLS use the provider\[cq]s port (usually 143) and
\f[CR]transport starttls\f[R].
Set \f[CR]certfile /path/to/ca.pem\f[R] for a private CA.
Set the certificate policy explicitly.
Legacy \f[CR]cert ignore\f[R] disables identity checks and
\f[CR]transport plain\f[R] sends unencrypted credentials.
Python 3.9+ is required, with sqlite3 for downloads.
\f[CR]JARO_IMAP_TIMEOUT\f[R] sets the network deadline in seconds
(1\[en]300, default 30).
.PP
At the prompt, \f[CR]help\f[R] lists commands and \f[CR]quit\f[R] or
Ctrl\-D exits.
The examples use a disposable account with a dot hierarchy, three
messages in \f[CR]Projects.Café team\f[R], and a folder named
\f[CR]Projects.List\-Dev\f[R].
Start at the logical root.
A slash separates components for every server delimiter.
A flat folder has no children.
Copy the displayed percent escapes for literal slashes, wildcards and
other special characters.
A space can be written as \f[CR]%20\f[R] or included inside quotes.
\f[CR][container]\f[R] marks a navigable folder that cannot hold
messages.
.IP
.EX
folders
cd /Projects
folders
cd \[aq]Café team\[aq]
pwd
ls \-\-messages \-\-limit 2
more
du /Projects \-\-depth 1 \-\-top 3
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]ls\f[R] shows children and a page of headers.
\f[CR]ls \-\-folders\f[R] lists only children.
The page holds 50 messages by default (1\[en]500).
Highest UID appears first.
The displayed Date header can be older or newer than its neighbors.
A missing or invalid date falls back to INTERNALDATE.
\f[CR]more\f[R] continues the same inventory boundary.
Run \f[CR]ls\f[R] again for new arrivals or after a UIDVALIDITY change.
\f[CR]refresh\f[R] rereads children and resets paging.
.PP
\f[CR]du\f[R] uses server\-reported message bytes where available.
Its fallback and \f[CR]\-\-exact\f[R] read size metadata.
\f[CR]\-\-top\f[R] ranks direct mailbox bytes.
The subtree column sums descendants within the requested depth.
Partial totals give lower bounds.
Shared copies can count twice.
Account quota, filesystem use and compressed archive size have different
accounting.
.PP
From the IMAP prompt, write a new archive on a filesystem supporting
hard links.
Allow space for raw message bytes plus the compressed tar, inventory and
tar headers.
For incompressible mail, budget roughly twice the message bytes plus
overhead and extra headroom for many small messages.
Add \f[CR]\-\-recursive\f[R] to include descendants.
.IP
.EX
download \[aq]/Projects/Café team\[aq] \-\-output cafe\-team.tar.gz
.EE
.PP
After an interruption keep \f[CR].cafe\-team.tar.gz.incomplete\f[R] next
to the intended output.
Reconnect to the same account and repeat the same selection and path.
.IP
.EX
download \[aq]/Projects/Café team\[aq] \-\-output cafe\-team.tar.gz \-\-resume
.EE
.PP
Resume verifies completed messages locally and restarts any partial
message.
Missing captured UIDs or a replaced mailbox require a new filename and a
fresh inventory.
Publication verifies the archive and preserves an existing output.
In your system shell, extract your verified copy into a fresh private
directory and compare each original with the manifest.
.IP
.EX
umask 077
gzip \-t cafe\-team.tar.gz &&
tar \-tzf cafe\-team.tar.gz &&
mkdir verified\-copy &&
tar \-xzf cafe\-team.tar.gz \-C verified\-copy &&
(cd verified\-copy && python3 \- <<\[aq]PY\[aq]
import hashlib, json
with open(\[dq]manifest.jsonl\[dq], encoding=\[dq]utf\-8\[dq]) as manifest:
    for line in manifest:
        record = json.loads(line)
        if record[\[dq]type\[dq]] == \[dq]message\[dq]:
            digest, size = hashlib.sha256(), 0
            with open(record[\[dq]path\[dq]], \[dq]rb\[dq]) as message:
                for block in iter(lambda: message.read(131072), b\[dq]\[dq]):
                    size += len(block)
                    digest.update(block)
            if (size, digest.hexdigest()) != (record[\[dq]size\[dq]], record[\[dq]sha256\[dq]]):
                raise SystemExit(\[dq]Verification failed for \[dq] + record[\[dq]path\[dq]])
print(\[dq]All listed messages verified\[dq])
PY
)
.EE
.PP
The versioned JSON Lines manifest maps generated \f[CR].eml\f[R] members
to original folder names, UIDs, dates, flags, sizes and SHA\-256
digests.
After verifying the copy, the following IMAP commands reorganize the
disposable account.
\f[CR]mv\f[R] (alias \f[CR]rename\f[R]) takes a complete destination
whose parent already exists.
Each source\[cq]s descendants move with it.
.IP
.EX
mkdir /Projects/Lists
mv \[aq]/Projects/Café team\[aq] \[aq]/Projects/Café archive\[aq]
rename\-glob \[aq]/Projects/List\-*\[aq] \[aq]/Projects/Lists/{1}\[aq]
.EE
.PP
Read every old and new path in the preview.
To accept this mapping, replace \f[CR]ID\f[R] with the printed ID in
\f[CR]apply ID\f[R] in the same session, then type \f[CR]yes\f[R] on the
controlling terminal.
A refusal, drift or unknown reply stops the serial renames.
Inspect completed, failed, unknown and pending pairs before a fresh
preview.
.PP
For deletion practice, create a new empty folder in the disposable
account.
Issue the second command only after the first succeeds and you intend to
remove that exact folder.
.IP
.EX
mkdir /Projects/Practice
rmdir /Projects/Practice
.EE
.PP
Check the target and message count, then type \f[CR]yes\f[R] on the
controlling terminal.
Enter cancels.
Deletion includes messages.
Descendants require \f[CR]\-\-recursive\f[R], which lists the target set
before confirmation.
Scripted deletion requires deliberate \f[CR]\-\-yes\f[R] when no
controlling terminal is available.
Downloading never initiates deletion.
Inspect surviving and unknown paths before another write.
.PP
Piped input omits the prompt.
Header rows retain tab\-separated fields when redirected.
Progress and diagnostics go to stderr.
Batch commands continue after a recoverable failure.
Exit 1 at EOF or \f[CR]quit\f[R] records any failed, cancelled or
partial command, even when a later command succeeds.
A fatal network timeout exits 124 and a session interrupted by Ctrl\-C
exits 130.
.SS Message snapshots
\f[CR]snapshot create DEST\f[R] copy regular Maildir
\f[CR]new\f[R]/\f[CR]cur\f[R] messages and filename flags from
\f[CR]$JAROMAILDIR\f[R] into a new private destination outside the
source.
Stop all mail writers first; detected source changes fail creation.
This is a message\-only copy, excluding accounts, credentials,
configuration, indexes, temporary messages and delivery journals.
Symlinks and existing destinations are refused.
A failed capture may leave an incomplete private destination; choose a
fresh destination after inspecting/removing it.
.PP
\f[CR]snapshot verify SNAPSHOT\f[R] check the complete manifest and
every payload using sizes and SHA\-256.
Invalid paths, missing/altered messages and malformed manifests fail.
.PP
\f[CR]snapshot restore SNAPSHOT DEST\f[R] verify the full artifact,
stage and verify message copies privately on the destination filesystem,
then publish into an absent or owned empty mail root.
Existing populated roots and symlink paths are refused.
Keep snapshot/destination writers stopped until it returns.
Outbox messages go under \f[CR].jaro\-recovered\-outbox\-N/outbox\f[R],
outside the normal send queue; inspect and explicitly requeue individual
messages only after checking delivery history.
Accounts, credentials and configuration need separate recovery; rebuild
the mail index after setup.
Handled failures clean staging; a parent\-sync failure rolls back
publication.
Power loss or failed cleanup can require inspection before retrying into
a fresh root.
.PP
All three commands work without initialization or network access; global
options, including \f[CR]\-n\f[R], are unsupported.
Exit status is 0 on success, 1 for validation/storage failure, 2 for
invalid invocation, and 128+signal for a handled interruption.
No encryption, live snapshot or full configuration recovery is provided.
See the manual\[cq]s message snapshot section for the versioned artifact
format and consistency requirements.
.SS Recipient groups
Local recipient lists live in \f[CR]$JAROMAILDIR/Groups/\f[R] (for
example \f[CR]Mail/Groups/team\f[R]) and are addressed as
\f[CR]team\[at]jaromail.group\f[R].
The first line may set \f[CR]#mode individual\f[R],
\f[CR]#mode cc\f[R]/\f[CR]#mode carboncopy\f[R], or
\f[CR]#mode bcc\f[R]; when no \f[CR]#mode\f[R] is set, groups use
\f[CR]#mode individual\f[R].
.SS Account and secrets
\f[CR]passwd\f[R] set or update account password in the configured
keyring.
.PP
\f[CR]askpass\f[R] print account password resolved from keyring/prompt.
.PP
\f[CR]isonline\f[R] check network reachability.
.SS Search and indexing
\f[CR]index\f[R] update the search index.
.PP
\f[CR]search\f[R] run search queries over local mail archives.
.PP
\f[CR]header\f[R], \f[CR]headers\f[R] print mail headers.
.SS Addressbook and contacts
\f[CR]abook\f[R] edit addressbook.
.PP
\f[CR]addr\f[R], \f[CR]list\f[R] list addresses from addressbook.
.PP
\f[CR]extract\f[R], \f[CR]parse\f[R] extract addresses from supported
inputs.
.PP
\f[CR]import\f[R] import addresses from stdin.
.PP
\f[CR]export [vcard]\f[R] export the selected contact list as vCard
records.
.PP
\f[CR]learn\f[R], \f[CR]isknown\f[R], \f[CR]complete\f[R] contact
learning/query/completion helpers.
.SS Filtering and Sieve
\f[CR]update\f[R] install a newer stable JaroMail release into the
current Linux prefix.
.PP
\f[CR]fresh [MAIL_ROOT]\f[R] refresh filters and the existing search
index.
.PP
\f[CR]fresh\-filters [MAIL_ROOT]\f[R] rebuild local filtering cache and
regenerate Sieve.
.PP
\f[CR]fresh\-search [MAIL_ROOT]\f[R] refresh the existing notmuch index.
.PP
\f[CR]filter\f[R] apply filters to a maildir.
.PP
\f[CR]sieve\f[R] publish the shared filter generation and its portable
\f[CR]Filters.sieve\f[R] export.
.PP
The native Linux Sieve package also installs
\f[CR]jaro\-sieve\-filter [\-\-dry\-run] \-\-from INCOMING \-\-to ROOT SCRIPT\f[R].
It files messages from the source Maildir into the named mailboxes under
ROOT.
Give it absolute directories.
Share the exported source with a Sieve\-enabled delivery server so that
each host compiles its own binary.
.PP
\f[CR]sieve\-import FILE\f[R] import a Jaromail\-generated Sieve file
into local filter/addressbook files.
The bounded token parser validates and stages the complete import before
publication.
\f[CR]\-n\f[R] validates without writes;
\f[CR]\-\-legacy\-generated\f[R] explicitly recovers older generated
header\-contains scripts with a migration diagnostic.
Arbitrary provider scripts are unsupported.
See the import contract for supported grammar, profile/mapping
requirements and recovery behavior.
.SS Maildir and utility commands
\f[CR]backup\f[R] move search results between maildirs.
.PP
\f[CR]merge\f[R] merge one maildir into another.
.PP
\f[CR]deliver\f[R] deliver a message from stdin into a maildir.
.PP
\f[CR]dedup [\-\-scratch\-dir DIR] [\-\-] MAILDIR...\f[R] removes extra
copies of messages whose stored bytes match exactly, including
duplicates in a single Maildir.
The first listed folder wins.
Within it, \f[CR]cur\f[R] wins over \f[CR]new\f[R], then the smallest
filename in unsigned byte order wins.
The survivor keeps its name and flags.
Use \f[CR]jaro \-n dedup Primary Archive\f[R] to preview each proposed
keep/remove pair.
Pause clients that move or edit existing entries during apply.
Delivery under fresh names may continue.
See the user manual for scratch placement, output escaping, accounting
and retry behavior.
.PP
\f[CR]rmdupes\f[R] uses the legacy \f[CR]formail\f[R] Message\-ID cache.
\f[CR]rmdupes lastlog\f[R] obtains its list of destinations from
\f[CR]maildirs_lastlog\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]remember\f[R], \f[CR]replay\f[R], \f[CR]preview\f[R],
\f[CR]edit\f[R], \f[CR]vim\f[R], \f[CR]imap\f[R], \f[CR]alot\f[R],
\f[CR]alot\-config\f[R], \f[CR]notmuch\f[R], \f[CR]notmuch\-config\f[R],
\f[CR]isml\f[R], \f[CR]ismd\f[R].
.SS Files
\f[CR]$JAROMAILDIR\f[R] mail root (default usually
\f[CR]$HOME/Mail\f[R]).
.PP
\f[CR]$JAROMAILDIR/Accounts/\f[R] account configuration files.
.PP
\f[CR]$JAROMAILDIR/Groups/\f[R] local recipient group files.
.PP
\f[CR]$JAROMAILDIR/Filters.txt\f[R] filtering rules.
.PP
\f[CR]$JAROMAILDIR/Filters.sieve\f[R] generated Sieve rules.
